								<ul id="learnmore-map">
									<li>Hover over any region on the map to see the estimated percentage of the advertiser’s audience from that region during the selected date range.</li>
									<li>For each ad on politics, elections, or social issues, Meta provides a breakdown of the audience percentage per region and the estimated number of impressions overall.</li>
									<li>However, because Meta does not provide a single overall audience percentage per region for all ads run by an advertiser, we performed additional calculations to tease out this crucial information.</li>
									<li>
										Suppose an advertiser ran <strong style="font-weight: bold">two ads</strong>:
										<ul>
											<li><strong style="font-weight: bold">Ad #1</strong>: <strong style="font-weight: bold">1,000</strong> impressions (50% Ontario, 50% Alberta)
												<ul>
													<li>Ontario: <strong style="font-weight: bold">1,000</strong> x 50% = <strong style="font-weight: bold">500</strong> impressions</li>
													<li>Alberta: <strong style="font-weight: bold">1,000</strong> x 50% = <strong style="font-weight: bold">500</strong> impressions</li>
												</ul>
											</li>
											<li><strong style="font-weight: bold">Ad #2</strong>: <strong style="font-weight: bold"></strong>100,000 impressions (80% Ontario, 20% Alberta)
												<ul>
													<li>Ontario: <strong style="font-weight: bold">100,000</strong> x 80% = <strong style="font-weight: bold">80,000</strong> impressions</li>
													<li>Alberta: <strong style="font-weight: bold">100,000</strong> x 20% = <strong style="font-weight: bold">20,000</strong> impressions</li>
												</ul>
											</li>
											<li>Total regional impressions:
												<ul>
													<li>Ontario: <strong style="font-weight: bold">500</strong> + <strong style="font-weight: bold">80,000</strong> = <strong style="font-weight: bold">80,500</strong> impressions</li>
													<li>Alberta: <strong style="font-weight: bold">500</strong> + <strong style="font-weight: bold">20,000</strong> = <strong style="font-weight: bold">20,500</strong> impressions</li>
												</ul>
											</li>
											<li> Overall percentages:
												<ul>
													<li>Total impressions: <strong style="font-weight: bold">1,000</strong> + <strong style="font-weight: bold">100,000</strong> = 101,000</li>
													<li>Ontario: <strong style="font-weight: bold">80,500</strong> / 101,000 = <strong style="font-weight: bold">79.7%</strong></li>
													<li>Alberta: <strong style="font-weight: bold">20,500</strong> / 101,000 = <strong style="font-weight: bold">20.3%</strong></li>
												</ul>
											</li>
										</ul>
									</li>
